# extant, in {{PROJECT}}

Documentation makes claims - a version, a file path, a commit, a branch - and
those claims decay. You cannot tell an expired line from a current one by
reading it, and neither can any other agent. This checks them against git.

Run it before you trust a document, and after you edit one.

## Check the documentation

```
python tools/extant_collect.py --verify
```

Exit 0 means every falsifiable claim in `{{DOC}}` still holds. Exit 1 lists what
does not, with line numbers.

To check one file:

```
python tools/extant_collect.py --validate path/to/file.md
```

## Check a draft BEFORE you write it

"Run it after you edit one" is too late if the edit was wrong. When you have
composed a document and not yet written it, pipe it in:

```
python tools/extant_collect.py --check-text --as-path docs/plan.md < draft.md
```

Same rules, same denominator, same exit code as `--validate`. It writes no
file. Pass `--as-path` with the path the document WOULD have: without it the
rules that key on a filename cannot answer, relative links resolve against the
repository root instead of the document's own directory, and the markup is
assumed to be markdown. The run says so on its own line when you leave it off,
and that line means the check was narrower than usual - not that the document
was clean.

## Read the denominator, not just the verdict

Every run ends with a line counting what it EXAMINED:

```
checked {{DOC}}: dead-sha 36, false-merge-claim 2, dead-md-link 5
```

A rule showing `0` found nothing to look at. That usually means a pattern is
wrong rather than that the document is clean, and the run says so on a `NOTE:`
line. Treat it as "investigate", never as "fine". A clean exit code from a rule
that examined nothing is the failure this tool exists to prevent.

## Never make a document pass by deleting the claim

If a finding is real, fix the claim or remove the thing that made it false.
Deleting the sentence also makes the run pass, and the tool cannot tell the two
apart. If you do delete something, say so in your report: a silent deletion is
indistinguishable from a repair, and the next reader inherits the difference.

## What it will not tell you

It never judges whether a number or a date is correct. "We had 2238 tests in
March" was true in March. It is not wrong now, only old, and there is nothing to
check it against. Do not add rules for that, and do not treat their absence as a
gap.

## Prove the checks work before relying on them

```
python tools/extant_collect.py --selftest
```

Breaks one real claim per rule, confirms the rule notices, and puts it back. A
rule that stays silent after its own probe is broken, whatever the exit code
said a moment ago.
